{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains","id":123404,"title":"In which I read Mortal Remains","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-01T14:01:37-07:00","id":123404,"title":"In which I read Mortal Remains","content":"So I'm going to try my hand at something I've seen around here a couple of times - a lets read. I'm aiming for a chapter every couple of days, and in between that you're welcome to discuss, review my review, make bets on what I'll think based on my &quot;infamous&quot; opinions or whatever.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Introduction</span><br /> <br /> The introduction was straight forward and introduced the book. I don't have anything to say about it, good or bad.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Pieces of Flesh.</span><br /> <br /> Ah, Promethean. The flawed masterpiece of the WoD. The section begins with a bit on xenophobia, \u201cthey are not our people\u201d which is very appropriate as Prometheans are very much the \u201cother\u201d in \u201cfear of the other\u201d. I could probably do a whole post on how Promethians embody this theme, and how it contrasts &amp; compliments Genius' fear of the new, but I shall resist the temptation and press on.<br /> <br /> One thing I noticed is that the xenophobia was tied into alienation and paranoia. At a guess this was an attempt to tie it into core hunter themes, though I'm not sure this was the best choice. Xenophobia can also unite people, sad but true, and in Promethean that's what it so often does. A community affected by disquiet are all on the same \u201cwavelength\u201d - and unity/teamwork are Hunter themes too. Still, this is a small niggle in an otherwise themic and appropriate opening.<br /> <br /> The Enemy: Mad Science and Old Legends<br /> <br /> Next we open up into an overview of what the created are. This bit is functional and solid, it covers how the Created interact with humanity, and why that causes appropriate problems for Hunters to solve. This is followed by a section on common misconceptions that hunters might fall into, which is frankly a great idea. If this was a full length book having a list of common misconceptions would, in my humble opinion, be a bit limiting to the sheer diversity of the Promethean concept. Night Stalkers did include far more than just the Kindred. But with Pieces of Flesh's laser focus on the reanimated corpses of the Created corebook it works great.<br /> <br /> One thing I was very surprised to see is that it says there is less than Created alive at any one time. This seems bizarre, considering that a typical game of created includes 3-5 PCs travelling together and plenty of NPC Created. One of the sample adventures includes 20 Created all in the same place, are we to assume the world's entire Created population is in a single region?<br /> <br /> Now we get to a section on Lineages. This is again straight forward and functional, having already read and played Promethian I'm sure I'm not the target reader for this section so I'll quickly move along.<br /> <br /> Following that we get a selection of different themes for Promethean: Body Horror, Obsession and The Creator. I think this section really suffers from it's limited word-count, themes are a tricky thing and this came across as more of a list than guidance on how to use them. Then again, if they're deftly woven into the later material then it won't matter.<br /> <br /> The three themes presented naturally not a comprehensive list of Created's themes, and I was surprised by the omission of divinity. Not only is it a core Created theme it also an extremely appropriate one to focus on for Hunter \u2013 xenophobia was the opening to the whole chapter, how entwined have xenophobia and religion been across history? And how many Compacts/Conspiracies are religious in nature.<br /> <br /> <br /> We now come onto mechanics for the Created; which being mechanics I shall skip over. Save to notice that disquiet is significantly rarer, and requires actual interaction or a supernatural or hostile display from the Promethean. Wastelands too. The advice for presenting Disquiet is also damn good \u2013 though the advice under \u201cShow don't Tell\u201d doesn't quite match up with the types of disquiet mentioned earlier.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">The response</span><br /> <br /> Compacts opens with the logical conclusion that the rarity of the Created is a big problem, it also points out that Mary Shelly's Frakenstein is not a howto for monster hunting. The next bit seems a bit less logical \u2013 that each organisation will have at least something on the Created, with so few it requires some suspension of disbelief. Requesting we leave our disbelief behind is probably fine, but I can't help but think a larger Created population would work better. The third suggestion I didn't really buy; that is, that it might make more thematic sense for hunters to be wrong about the Created. Mistakes are always in theme, but I can't see why this is more appropraite for the Created rather than, say, the fae.<br /> <br /> <br /> Ashwood Abbey \u2013 This starts with the implication that a Created is a better human than the Abbey, sensible. The primary theme of this section is that the Created's potential for redemption is a threat to the Abbey's thin justifications. This is a good angle, especially since there's not much room to link the Created to the Abbey's theme of so called \u201cclass\u201d. Also \u2013 how on Earth did Ogilvy survive the ending fiction?<br /> <br /> The Long Night \u2013 The section opens badly, by saying that The Long Night think inhuman monsters like vampires deserve only death, this directly contradicts Hunter core. It's also an odd choice of opening for this section as the distinction, and where Created's potential for humanity places them, is quickly declared irrelevant by the Long Night not knowing of the pilgrimage. I think this section could have been done much better. On the closing fiction \u2013 how can the Long Night not believe in Ghosts when they're the most common supernatural creature?<br /> <br /> The Loyalists of Thule \u2013 This one I liked. It's a bit straight forward, of course the Loyalists will be cerebral, interested in secrets, and curious about how Promethians are created. But with three paragraphs there's not much room for more than straight forward.<br /> <br /> Network Zero \u2013 I feel about this the same as the Loyalists. It's straight forward but with so little word count what else could it be. That said, I never liked the \u201cviral marketing\u201d excuse. I much prefer willing disbelief as Net0's arch-enemy.<br /> <br /> Null Mysteriis \u2013 Since this is Promethian (and the word count is expanded) I expected a lot from the Null Mysteriis section; it did not disappoint. It began with a very strong opening \u201cNull Mysteriis has discovered disquiet\u201d (though the Loyalists haven't even read the publications?). The next section goes onto their speculation about the nature of disquiet, they're wrong but in a way that's both plausible and clever. I tip my hat. I particularly like the way Imp was wrong to think so little of O.R.A.S.<br /> <br /> The Union \u2013 We have the longest word-count yet this fits the earlier pattern of straight forward and solid. The ending fiction made me think of surfer-dudes. Surfer dude union is a hilarious image that's lightened my evening with giggles.<br /> <br /> <br /> Closing thoughts \u2013 \u201cStraight forward and solid\u201d summarises my thoughts quite well. Aside from a misstep with The Long Night, these are for the most part exactly what I'd expect from Hunter \u2013 Promethian interactions. Network Zero films them, but no one believes them. The Union defend their turf, and only deal with Promethians who make trouble or disquiet.<br /> <br /> Null Mysteriis is probably the most important compact here, given it's thematic ties to Promethian so I'm pleased to say it was the one I liked the most. Honestly I would have liked to see more of what it did \u2013 talk about the non-obvious stuff connected to the obvious interactions. I can figure out for myself that Null Mysteriis will study Promethinas, and while I probably could come up with some interesting theories for them, I wouldn't have come up with the ones in this book so I'm glad someone else did.<br /> <br /> Finally. I know I said I wasn't going to talk about the fiction, but I have to say I loved the Promethian's journals. Is it a bad thing if the Promethians are better than the Hunters in a Hunter book? Probably not <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Conspiracies</span><br /> <br /> Agis Kai Doru \u2013 The gist of the this section is that the closet thing to Relics asssociated with the Created are Azothic objects and dormant Pandorans; but that the AKD don't particularly focus on either. This is probably the strongest link you could make given the contents of the Promethian line, but I can't help but feel this is a missed opportunity. Since the AKD aren't too interested in the existing relics, I felt, as a player, a similar disinterest \u2013 so why not use the space instead to talk about evocative Demiurge mad mad science relics that you'd get excited to collect for the AKD?<br /> <br /> Orpheus' Eye is a solid Endowment, but apart from it's origin story it has little to do with the Created.<br /> <br /> Ascending Ones \u2013 We've had the scientists, now we're onto the Alchemists. Nothing about this captured me quite like the creative look at disquiet under Null Mysteriis; but I did like the merciful angle and their positive view on the new dawn is very fitting. I would have liked more about the Southern Temple and alchemists in general; but I suspect the Knife of Paradise and protection would be easier to fit into a word-count. I would describe this as a good start, that the deeper links could be based upon, which is why I did dislike the explicit mention that their knowledge is limited, I would rather that the knowledge was contained in \u201cadvanced texts\u201d that most AO hunters just aren't interested in reading. (It could be read that way, but it wasn't the impression I got)<br /> <br /> Gentle Mind \u2013 an anti-disquiet Elixir. I like the concept but I'm not too sold on the price, for such a narrow concept I'd say one dot. Also it only helps against ties, my guesstimate on the Disquiet rolls is that mortals will typically have a better pool, but you roll so often that you have to watch out for the occasional freak roll. Protecting against ties doesn't really fit that paradigm. I'll probably change this to a single auto-success.<br /> <br /> The Cheiron Group \u2013 We're now back to straight forward presentations. I agree that TCG is are the ones likely to realise the underlying biologial similarities between Lineages, I wonder if they feel the same about the Unfleshed? The lack of a Cherion are mad scientists angle was surprising though.<br /> <br /> Vitriol Pump \u2013 a nifty idea. I was surprised this doesn't cause side effects, but then Cheiron has already used more dangerous stuff than the Divine Fire (like the Infernal fire).<br /> <br /> Lucifuge \u2013 I felt underwhelmed by this section. We see one of the rare peeks at the broader gameline, this being the qashmallim-angel / Lucifuge-demon connection which was clearly too tempting to ignore. But we have Lucifuge directly confronted by angels and... they decide to tread carefully and consider the created on a case by case basis? In Created the Qashmallim, even when they weren't actually that impressive, still felt awesome. In the Hunter core the Lucifuge mentions Qashmallim, and it makes it clear that meeting one is a massive deal. Here they seem to be closer to \u201ceh that's interesting.\u201d.<br /> <br /> Gulf of Hades is a neat one. It ties into the Promethian and Mad Science angle well. I'm less fond of the lethal damage it does to humans, it doesn't really fit the theme of draining energy. If it shut down machines, drained Pyros from Promethians and Willpower from Humans; that would be perfect. I'm not sure what I'd prefer in Hunter's mechanics though.<br /> <br /> The Malleus Maleficarum \u2013 So once again we have the Inquisition assuming the worst and burning people. No surprises there, I think you could do a lot of interesting stuff with the juxtaposition of \u201cundead monsters\u201d from their point of view with Angels, but alas. Word Count.<br /> <br /> The Peace of St Joseph is a good Benediction, it's a little odd seing a Benediciton with a penalty against Prometheans in the Promethian section but I quite like it.<br /> <br /> Task Force: VALKYRIE \u2013 the first sentence opened badly. Not having to do their own legwork, getting orders, to me that's should be the defining difference between a Compact and a Conspiracy. I know the line didn't go that way, but still: The Malleus, Cheiron Group, Ageis Kai Doru and VASCU should get quite a lot of legwork done for them. VASCU often don't hit the field until a sheriff called in with lists of killings, modeus operandi, witness statements, etc. I really like the fact this is talking about TF:V as professionals, not four dudes with big guns doing their own thing, I just dislike the fact it's saying that's unusual and unique.<br /> <br /> The middle bit about Disquiet making it personal is fine but the bit about the Brass not giving proper instructions is just silly. I can accept that TF:V wants Prometheans alive (though what's that \u201cmysterious forces\u201d that is mentioned in a throw away line?) but sending the troops out with no info is a way to loose your men and a extremely rare and valuable opportunity to take a Promethian alive. You'd be far better off giving all the tactical information you know, and trusting your men to obey thir orders. Give them incendiary rounds, normal bullets won't take a Promethean's leg off but fire will. A Promethian can survive without it's legs, it can't run without them.<br /> <br /> Gleipnir Restraints are a neat idea. The maths works out too.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Wandering Monsters</span><br /> <br /> I don't really have much to say about these. The three Promethians presented felt like typical examples, they illustrate the game well without pushing it's limits; but when they're responsible for diaries that good, who cares? The exact same applies to the three story hooks, though I was pleased to see ackowladgement that multi-Compact/Conspiracy teams are uncommon.<br /> <br /> I notice that the Sources and Inspiration section does not include The Golem and the Djinn. I've only just started the book, but I can say it really should be on this list.<br /> <br /> <br /> Conclusions<br /> <br /> Apart from a few sour notes I felt this was solid, but I think the word-count really constrained it. This section tends towards straight forward portrayals of hunter-Promethian relationships, while omitting some of the richer thematic parallels between the underlying themes (which admittedly need more words): Hunters faith and Promethian's Quashamalim. Mad Science vs Hunter's reactionary streak. The conflict between base urges and higher principles/Principles. Even xenopohbia barely appeared once you got to The Response. The best part though, was clearly the fiction which was basically sublime.<br /> <br /> I remember when Mortal Remains was in early development Matthew asked what we would like to see; one of the suggestions was an organisation of Rabbis. Matt was hesitant to write a group based on Judaism and I encouraged it; having seen this I think he made the right call to omitt it. There's so much you can do with Judaism-Promethians \u2013 old to young, tradition to blank slates, promised land to pilgrims, and that's before you get to the Rabbi of Prague. I don't think it would have been be a good idea to do it without enough word-count to do it justice.<br /> <br /> <br /> And that's it until next time. Feel free to discuss, flame me, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/561-conspiracy-hakollel-shel-hamerkava-the-secret-names-of-god\">tear apart my attempts at Promethian Hunters</a> while saying stuff about glass houses. Tune in (probably) on Saturday for Changelings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T14:32:30-07:00","id":124770,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>All is Lost</b></span><br /> <br /> The chapter opens with the theme of paranoia. This is an interesting choice, much like Promethian's xenophobia. It's a theme shared by both the Hunter and the Changeling gameline.<br /> <br /> The reason I say it's an interesting choice is because the better a fae is at hiding among humanity, the less threatening it is to hunters. Fae doing nasty hunter-disaproved things tends to damage their sanity, and often in particularly faeish ways. Obviously most hunters won't know that, but it makes it an unusual choice at least in my opinion. If only because vampires are better at being evil and hidden.<br /> <br /> Following on we have a brief couple of paragraphs about fairy tales; this includes the suggestion that the Lost run by rules of an alien reality which is good; the suggestion that faerie tales are advice on how to survive that reality \u00e2\u20ac\u201c I have mixed feelings. Faerie tales were cautionary tales, but for how to survive in our mundane world. It does make sense to assume that in the WoD they really are designed as cautionary tales for the supernatural, though that would (according to the rules in Changeling: the Lost) imply that WoD faerie tales are either really badly designed cautionary tales or completely different to the ones we read. Either way could work well, but it would have benefited from a discussion on the implications.<br /> <br /> Closing the section of is three rules; the final one (don't make deals) I think was actually a mistake. Firstly because the actual rule in faerie tales is usually \u201cdon't break deals\u201d, consider The Three Spinners for example. Changeling too follows this theme. Make a deal with a vampire and it will most probably break it's word and suck you dry the moment it feels advantageous to do so. Make a deal with a Changeling and 9/10 times it won't break it. In other words, if your deal with a vampire goes bad you shouldn't have made a deal with a vampire. If your deal with a fae goes bad, you should get a better lawyer.<br /> <br /> The second reason is that deals with the fae are interesting stories. Because they don't break their deals the fae are a great resource for someone who wants something from the supernatural (other than it's hide on a platter). The Hunter who only wants the leprechaun's pot of gold has a lot of precedent, it would be a shame if he wasn't covered in this section.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The Lost</b></span><br /> <br /> As before I shall not dwell much on the section about Changeling: the Lost, it seemed like a competent summery, but targeted at people who \u2013 unlike me \u2013 haven't played Changeling so I have nothing much to say.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The Response</b></span><br /> <br /> <b>Ashwood Abbey</b> \u2013 The general gist is that most faeries aren't impressive enough to hunt, but some are and others make good suppliers. This is good, but the bit about hunting through the Hedge was weird. The hedge is supremely dangerous, and being psychoractive makes it more dangerous for the Abbey than most people. The Abbey aren't known for their sense of restraint, but natural selection should have removed that idea.<br /> <br /> <b>The Long Night</b> \u2013 We start with a Christian lens, which is only natural for the Long Night, based around the biblical Nephilim. I'm not the Nephilim in the bible were ever considered evil, but they were fallen angels. As the Long Night believe that the True Fae are trying to corrupt humanity \u2013 the more powerful and fae-like a Changeling is, the more corrupt it is. My biggest gripe with this is that The Long Night agree with each other. It's a good theology and I can certainly see a cell of the Long Night preaching it, but I've always understood that every long night Cell had their own spin on the core doctrine and sometimes vastly different interpretations on specific points (Wikipedia's page on faeries lists quite a few Christian interpretations)<br /> <br /> <b>The Loyalists of Thule</b> \u2013 The first two I liked, with caveats. This one I don't really like. The summery is that the Loyalists believe that the True Fae are gods (a simplification, but correct) and fear that if provoked they might attack and destroy humanity. Caution is the party line for the loyalists, but being the best academics of the core compacts they should at the very least have figured out that fairy tales are instruction manuals for interacting with the fae, as said in the chapter's opening section, which in turn means that if it was easy to provoke a genocide it would have happened already.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that this idea is a bad one. Rather, it needs more explanation than we got. Why did they favour this piece of evidence over that and come to this conclusion? I also think that since the PCs are clearly the younger Loyalists mentioned as having defied the order the limited word-count might be better placed on what they think \u2013 what theories are popular, etc.<br /> <br /> <b>Network Zero</b> \u2013 You know, I had completely forgotten Radio Free Fae existed. I think this is a great way to work with limited word-count, we all know that Network Zero will want to put faeries on youtube, so pointing out these more obscure links between the settings is more useful. I'm a bit surprised Radio Free Fae don't know who Net0 is though, I thought Net0 advertised just about everything but their names.<br /> <br /> <b>Null Mysteriis</b> \u2013 And another one I really didn't like. Take this quote \u201cWhen first hand reports surface, Null sci-<br /> entists write off the unexplicable,\u201d. This is exactly what Null Mysteriis don't do. That's what separates O.R.A.S. from other scientists. They might be less interested in dream manipulation than hedge gates or an Ogre's super-strength but they would not write it off without testing it.<br /> <br /> The section from the archives even reads like a scientist trying to rationalise away obvious evidence by furiously making up nonsense justifications. Trace drugs do not noticeably affect nearby people. You do not need a control group for hedge gates \u2013 control groups are designed to minimise confounding variables. There is are no confounding variables for Hedge gates.<br /> <br /> <b>The Union</b> \u2013 This is pretty much what you'd expect. Union cells tend to notice when lots of weird strangers come into their neighbourhoods and if the fae keep quiet they don't really care.<br /> <br /> <b>Aegis Kai Doru</b> \u2013 Another one with few surprises. The AKD want the fae's relics, and if a fae can make relics they'd want the supply. The fae are not the AKD's primary enemy but sometimes a hunter confuses them for a Witch or a Werewolf or interprets their vow broadly. I didn't like the bit about \u201can all-out war between the Conspiracy and a Freehold\u201d. The AKD is a sprawling ancient organisation with access to literal WMDs. Freeholds are small, chaotic, and disorganised. In an all-out war it should be a decisive victory for the AKD; Tier 3 vs Tier 2.<br /> <br /> The Silver Key is a straight forward relic that gives access to the Hedge. I particularly like the way it's explicitly something the AKD has lots of, a few more of those would be a good idea. The whole AKD and the Hedge could have used more expansion though, the Hedge is insanely dangerous, deliberately impossible for humans to navigate. So the AKD would still be enamoured but might have a more nuanced view that would be worth expanding on.<br /> <br /> <b>The Ascending Ones</b> \u2013 So how does a cell of Ascending Ones manage to take down a True Fae more than once? They don't mind Changelings keeping to themselves. They don't like True Fae kidnapping. That all makes sense, it's classic Ascending One motives. But what makes them so successful? Apart from that it's solid, covering their views on Changelings, True Fae and the obvious use of Goblin Fruit in Elixirs.<br /> <br /> The Hound Mark is a good Elixir, it's use is duplicated by Red Resin but both work slightly differently. Hound Mark is short range but specific, Red Resin is long range but vague. I would have liked some explanation for why the Fae are easier to detect, the Mask is supposed to be one of the strongest illusions. Mentioning that it's made from goblin fruit (it probably is, but it doesn't say) would do it.<br /> <br /> <b>The Cherion Group</b> \u2013 This section is mostly history. So it raises a few questions like how they found the fetches they experimented on, but on the whole it's a sensible history. Also mentioned is that they really want an ability to telepathically communicate and influence people (changeling's dream powers) and that they're getting a supply of hedgebeast and hob bodies from somewhere mysterious. (The story then reveals that it's Goblin Markets, I guessed privateers but both make sense)<br /> <br /> Cranial Cortex Augmentation; so lost brains give you the ability to physically transform into a dream-being? Actually, this is faeries, that makes as much sense as anything else. It's not stated why Cheiron wanted dream powers so badly, but given Field Research and Recruitment shenanigans I'm sure players can think of a good use for this.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>The Lucifuge</b> \u2013 I always felt the Lucifuge should generally be better at telling what actually is a demon, but since a True Fae disguised as an angel or demon should have not trouble fooling a hunter, so I'm fine with the idea that the Lucifuge going after \u201cdemonic\u201d True Fae. On the whole they're concerned with this world and not the hedge, and consider Changeling\u2019s to not be making too much trouble. But they also believe the True Fae are powerful enough to be cosmologically significant and wonder what's up and the Lady has some vague goal that requires knowing more about the True Fae. All in all, standard Lucifgue.<br /> <br /> Unholy Aura opens with the oddest statement possible. \u201cBetter to reign in hell than serve in heaven, and the Lucifuge agree\u201d. Um, no they don't. They hate what they are. Apart from that this is a cool concept, the royalty of hell getting some general social bonuses because, we'll they're royalty. I like it, I don't like the justification for this being extra effective against the Fae but I do like the fact True Fae pay extra attention to anyone using this Castigation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Malleus Maleficarum</b> \u2013 I think that if you're in the core 12, you need to have support for PCs going up against any kind of monster. While Changelings aren't vampires, there's plenty of angles that could justify it; to start with two of the groups disagree with Baudolino's monomania for Vampires. It also mentions that the Malleus has no position on the fae so it's hunters fall back to whatever they got from their own upbringing. If the MM doesn't have anything why not fall back to the Church, they'll have something due to the church's tendency to syncretize just about everything everything. If you want to play a faerie hunting Irish priest, I think this section needs to do a better job of supporting you.<br /> <br /> Saint Collen\u2019s Clarity is basically an improved version of the True Sight of St Able. It works on both active and passive powers, instead of just passive, and it automatically beats everything. I felt that the True Sight was better balanced, I'm not fond of powers that work uncontested. I'll probably rework this into a power that undoes active deceptions and has no effects on passive ones. That would give both it and the True Sight their own useful niche.<br /> <br /> <b>Task Force: Valkarie</b> \u2013 So TFV is basically working with the faeries. TWILIGHT just spy on them and conclude they're no threat to humanity. FORT study their magic, and ADAMANSKI works with radio free fae to keep the fae under cover. This isn't as bad as it sounds, TWILIGHT is right that faeries aren't a threat \u2013 and with the ability to supply legitimate ID they have a lot of room at the negotiating table. However it could really benefit from more support for actual combat, what will convince TFV that this particular faerie is a threat and let some PCs break out guns.<br /> <br /> Compound Rounds are a great idea conceptually, I'm actually surprised that they weren't in the corebook. But like Saint Collen's Clarity we have a power that, as written, always works. I'd have preferred it if it only works on monsters vulnerable to one of it's component materials.<br /> <br /> <br /> Closing thoughts: Is it possible for me to think that hunters are too nice? Virtually nobody here actually hunts Changelings; now on the one hand I always felt that the \u201cxenophobic burn-em-all hunter\u201d was a stereotype that needs to die. That it sells the diversity and richness of vigil short. At the same time, the diversity of Hunter does include some good-ol' xenophobes.<br /> <br /> Either Clare was too damn nice to have humans shooting at Changelings after what happened in Arcadia or maybe there wasn't word-count to cover both the cooperative side and the antagonistic side; but either way I felt the lack of support for hunter vs changeling quite strongly.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Wondering Monsters</b></span><br /> <br /> Coming up with only three Changelings to illustrate the breadth of Lost is a hard challenge, but the three here do the job. You have the someone who's more like a consequence for faerie tale rules, someone who's more of a normal person, and someone who's a bit more of a player in the occult game with his Keeper hanging over his shoulder.<br /> <br /> It promises that each one can be an ally or an enemy, a welcome move after the last section, and again it delivers. Tensare Custos, (the rules consequence) basically kills people who hurt women. Reading this made me realise something; I know from reading lost that his targets will be picked according to a legalistic criteria so you could have a great story your hunter cell kills a few evil witches and now have this guy bearing down on you; or some other case where by a human (or at least, your) moral code he's the enemy. I don't think that would be obvious from reading this section alone, so that could have been added.<br /> <br /> The three story hooks were also good, providing a nice diversity beyond here's a monster kill it. Though I have to say, how did Conspiracies get so big and so old without knowing about Goblin Markets already?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T23:43:54-07:00","id":125035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=124770#post124770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Network Zero</b> \u2013 You know, I had completely forgotten Radio Free Fae existed. I think this is a great way to work with limited word-count, we all know that Network Zero will want to put faeries on youtube, so pointing out these more obscure links between the settings is more useful. I'm a bit surprised Radio Free Fae don't know who Net0 is though, I thought Net0 advertised just about everything but their names.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, the internet is a deep ocean and Network Zero is simply be all over the place. Vampire here, Werewolf there, etc. I'm pretty sure that the Winter Court knows about the Network but is keeping it under wraps.<br /> <br /> Do you really think that the court who's main MO is hiding is going to open it's self up to a hunter organization that is going to blast the identities of changelings all over the internet?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=124770#post124770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <b>The Ascending Ones</b> \u2013 So how does a cell of Ascending Ones manage to take down a True Fae more than once? They don't mind Changelings keeping to themselves. They don't like True Fae kidnapping. That all makes sense, it's classic Ascending One motives. But what makes them so successful? Apart from that it's solid, covering their views on Changelings, True Fae and the obvious use of Goblin Fruit in Elixirs.<br /> <br /> The Hound Mark is a good Elixir, it's use is duplicated by Red Resin but both work slightly differently. Hound Mark is short range but specific, Red Resin is long range but vague. I would have liked some explanation for why the Fae are easier to detect, the Mask is supposed to be one of the strongest illusions. Mentioning that it's made from goblin fruit (it probably is, but it doesn't say) would do it..</div> </div> </div> </div>My guess is an Elixir that turns one's hands or increases the iron count in blood, thus making one's blood terrifying for the Fae.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=124770#post124770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>The Cherion Group</b> \u2013 This section is mostly history. So it raises a few questions like how they found the fetches they experimented on, but on the whole it's a sensible history. Also mentioned is that they really want an ability to telepathically communicate and influence people (changeling's dream powers) and that they're getting a supply of hedgebeast and hob bodies from somewhere mysterious. (The story then reveals that it's Goblin Markets, I guessed privateers but both make sense)<br /> <br /> Cranial Cortex Augmentation; so lost brains give you the ability to physically transform into a dream-being? Actually, this is faeries, that makes as much sense as anything else. It's not stated why Cheiron wanted dream powers so badly, but given Field Research and Recruitment shenanigans I'm sure players can think of a good use for this..</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9PJNlmk5Yik\" data-code=\"9PJNlmk5Yik\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/9PJNlmk5Yik/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=124770#post124770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <b>Task Force: Valkarie</b> \u2013 So TFV is basically working with the faeries. TWILIGHT just spy on them and conclude they're no threat to humanity. FORT study their magic, and ADAMANSKI works with radio free fae to keep the fae under cover. This isn't as bad as it sounds, TWILIGHT is right that faeries aren't a threat \u2013 and with the ability to supply legitimate ID they have a lot of room at the negotiating table. However it could really benefit from more support for actual combat, what will convince TFV that this particular faerie is a threat and let some PCs break out guns.<br /> <br /> Compound Rounds are a great idea conceptually, I'm actually surprised that they weren't in the corebook. But like Saint Collen's Clarity we have a power that, as written, always works. I'd have preferred it if it only works on monsters vulnerable to one of it's component materials.</div> </div> </div> </div>Never understood why TF:V would be working with faeries. By in large, TF:V is a military response and I can easily see a few TF:V brass freaking out upon reports of imposters coming out of nowhere, killing people (and thus reducing them to piles of sticks, stones, and puppy dog tails), and starting to ease their way back into society. TF:V personal would be looking at the reports and pull a full McCarthy with alarm bells and everything.<br /> <br /> I could also see parts (who are more clued in) of the organization seeing changelings as abuse victims and suffering from PTSD (much akin to what soldiers and Hunters have to deal with). I can see the same part being hampered by bureaucratic red tape since TF:V mission is a military one and thus rehabbing victims isn't part of the job (beyond a little knock out juice).<br /> <br /> In short, the TF:V should deviate from &quot;What are these things coming in! where are they from? are they a foreign invasion force! And their killing people God-damn it!&quot; to &quot;Yes, we've checked your ID and you're a citizen but due to recent budget cuts, we are unable to supplement your sessions with your physiologist. Yes, we know about your special needs. we are currently trying to mov-.. Put the chair down now!&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=124770#post124770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Closing thoughts: Is it possible for me to think that hunters are too nice? Virtually nobody here actually hunts Changelings; now on the one hand I always felt that the \u201cxenophobic burn-em-all hunter\u201d was a stereotype that needs to die. That it sells the diversity and richness of vigil short. At the same time, the diversity of Hunter does include some good-ol' xenophobes.<br /> <br /> Either Clare was too damn nice to have humans shooting at Changelings after what happened in Arcadia or maybe there wasn't word-count to cover both the cooperative side and the antagonistic side; but either way I felt the lack of support for hunter vs changeling quite strongly.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nice isn't the word I was thinking of. Clued in and believing what changelings are saying is the major one. Hunter organizations exist in a mix between ignorance and knowledge and it seem like most of them are way too clued in Changelings as a whole. Hunter organizations should know about fetches, changelings, and the True Fae but not a lot of them should understand how the relationship works. Some may understand almost completely while others think that this is some hidden invasion force.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":604,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","user":"Shock","date":". 05-04-2014, 02:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-04T02:49:15-07:00","id":125091,"title":"","content":"Hey Shock.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=125035#post125035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you really think that the court who's main MO is hiding is going to open it's self up to a hunter organization that is going to blast the identities of changelings all over the internet?</div> </div> </div> </div> Open themselves up, no. Use them as a tool - feed them false information and let hunters worldwide watch it; or the location of a priviateer's safehose. That sounds like Winter's MO.<br /> <br /> That said, radio free fae can't be that secret since the whole idea is for changelings in other Freeholds (where communication lines are poor) to find it and tune in. So I suspect Winter would do more to &quot;sanitise&quot; RFF for public consumption than to try and keep Net0 away.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=125035#post125035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Never understood why TF:V would be working with faeries. By in large, TF:V is a military response and I can easily see a few TF:V brass freaking out upon reports of imposters coming out of nowhere, killing people (and thus reducing them to piles of sticks, stones, and puppy dog tails), and starting to ease their way back into society. TF:V personal would be looking at the reports and pull a full McCarthy with alarm bells and everything.</div> </div> </div> </div> Faeries have plenty of offer TFV, or anybody, I don't think there's much on an institutional level that TFV would want from the Lost, but individual agents cutting a deal - help me with this specific mission and I'll get you an ID - makes sense. TFV is about 1/3 a spy organisation so informants and under-the-table deals should be normal to them.<br /> <br /> Since the Lost are easier to bribe, and almost never break their contracts. They also make better trading partners than most monsters. Assuming you're trained in faerie negotiations, otherwise you're screwed.<br /> <br /> <br /> Apart from that; I think it comes down to personal taste. McCarthism would work (especially since the opening theme is Paranoia) but I personally prefer my Hunter Conspiracies knowledgeable. TF:V knows what Changelings are, here's why they hunt them anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shock</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/123404-in-which-i-read-mortal-remains?p=125035#post125035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nice isn't the word I was thinking of. Clued in and believing what changelings are saying is the major one. Hunter organizations exist in a mix between ignorance and knowledge and it seem like most of them are way too clued in Changelings as a whole. Hunter organizations should know about fetches, changelings, and the True Fae but not a lot of them should understand how the relationship works. Some may understand almost completely while others think that this is some hidden invasion force.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm generally ok with hunter conspiracies being clued-in. Which is probably why that angle didn't stand out to me as much. So a Conspiracy understanding how the True Fae/Fetch/Changeling relationship works; I'm ok with that. If for no other reason than I like to play up the differences between the tiers.<br /> <br /> But even a Hunter who knows exactly what a Changeling is can have plenty of reasons to hunt one, that angle seemed to be missing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":134,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","user":"The Kings Raven","date":". 05-04-2014, 05:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-05T15:39:39-07:00","id":126182,"title":"","content":"<b>Ghostwalkers</b><br /> <br /> We open with an overview of Sin-Eaters and Sin-Eater history. The general gist of it is that Ancient Egypt\u2019s obsession with all things death points to the fact Sin-Eaters were highly influential at the time. Afterwards a cultural change makes Sin-Eaters harder to notice.<br /> <br /> This is probably wrong - I suspect Ancient Egypt got it\u2019s death obsession from Irem; and being a very long time period full of death studying mystics might have gotten into a self-reinforcing loop (E.G. create Mekhet, oops, better learn more about death to do something about that). Plus, there couldn\u2019t be a unified Sin-Eater culture before modern communication.<br /> <br /> However it is a plausible mistake for hunters to make. And I can easily see five year old Krewes going \u201cYeah, we totally stretch back to Ancient Egypt. Praise Anubis the god of the dead!\u201d. So on the whole this was a nice bit of history. It\u2019s the sort of mistake I could see hunters making, sin-eaters making, and sin-eaters pretending to be true because it\u2019s totally pimpin.<br /> <br /> After we\u2019re done with ancient history we come to recent history and this section is gold. I was pleased that the short WWII fiction fit exactly how I said a WWII Hunter book should go (you\u2019ll notice quite a bit of WWII origin stories in my Hunter work), and basically comes with a lightning tour of cool places to set a Sin-Eater game: Victorian england occultism, World War battlefields, Eastern Europe in the aftermath of the Wars, communist Russia. Seriously, pretty much all of Europe is bound to still be dealing with the ghostly aftermath of the World Wars or Stalin. Geist needs more on the subject.<br /> <br /> We conclude the history with a brief section saying that in the present Ghost Walkers are rare and the hunters don\u2019t know much about them. This fits the Geist books, but much like the Geist books I always had to ask myself - what stopping Sin-Eaters going on TV?<br /> <br /> <br /> The next section is a long one about the relationships between Sin-Eaters and hunters, with several angels presented. There\u2019s nothing I particularly want to mention, other than I very much enjoyed this section. Obviously it was constrained by space, but it made full use of the words that were there.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>The Response</b><br /> <br /> Unlike the other sections there\u2019s no fiction snippets here. On the one hand Matt\u2019s promethian fiction was pure gold, on the other hand wordcount is limited. Lets see if this was the right choice.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Ashwood Abbey -</b> I wouldn\u2019t call it a \u201cmoral\u201d dilemma, but the Abbey has trouble deciding whether they prefer to party with or hunt Sin-Eaters. It\u2019s also mentioned that Sin-Eaters are starting to figure out what the abbey represents and want them dead. That\u2019s very plausible for Reapers, but maybe a touch more crossover than I\u2019m comfortable with as it implies Sin-Eaters changing another gameline\u2019s setting.<br /> <br /> <b>The Long Night -</b> Once again the Long Night seems to share a compact wide theology about a monster. Though as it ties directly into the one bit of theology that is definitely compact wide (the tribulation is now) it seems fitting, they believe Ghostwalkers are agents of the four Horsemen (where have we heard that before? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ) and sent to Earth to battle against God and co. The Merciful haven\u2019t given up hope of rescuing the \u201cpossessed\u201d humans, but most seem to think they are closer to demons. Basically they think that if the Devil\u2019s starting to deploy his big guns, Jesus is coming soon. (I was surprised that it\u2019s \u201cmore radical\u201d hunters who think their appearance was a good thing). I\u2019d also have liked an evangelical angle based around krewes. It feels like it would really be fitting if the Long Night and Sin-Eaters battled for souls, abstinence till marriage vs crazy sex parties for example.<br /> <br /> <b>The Loyalists of Thule -</b> As everyone suspected. Their outlook is heavily shaped by WWII. What does surprise me is that the best investigators of Tier 2 explicitly haven\u2019t learned anything since WWII. I would have prefered to see how they reconcile the vengeful dead of the war with New Orleans parties. This section also says that the Loyalists are exploring the Underworld, I\u2019ll never say no to more opportunities for Underworld gaming; I also liked the idea of the Loyalists recruiting native guides (Sin-Eaters after all are very bribable), but I didn\u2019t like the idea that they feel Sin-Eaters had a unique ability to navigate the Underworld since that\u2019s limiting and untrue (unless it means crossing rivers, but I don\u2019t think it did).<br /> <br /> <b>Network Zero -</b> Earlier on I asked what keeps Sin-Eaters of TV? This section sort of addresses it by saying it attracts the \u201cwrong attention\u201d, I say sort-of because not all Sin-Eaters will know that. The gist here is that Sin-Eaters willingness to be on camera means Net0 likes them, but a small minority tend to hurt Net0 for poking their noses where it doesn\u2019t belong. That seems fitting. Once again I didn\u2019t like the \u201cspecial effects\u201d justification, I much prefer willful denial.<br /> <br /> <b>Null Mysteriis -</b> It mentions at one point that the Null Mysteriis ask for volunteers, clearly they don\u2019t get ANY because some of their theories would be quickly disproved if they did. Basically Null Mysteriis believe that ghosts exist, that Sin-Eaters exist, and in the powers Sin-Eaters are reported to have. That\u2019s good. They also want to try and understand it, and are thinking along the lines of ectoplasm (Rationalist) or psychological (Open Minds) phenomena, makes sense.<br /> <br /> However they don\u2019t believe that Sin-Eater visit the underworld - a plausible belief on the assumption that they\u2019ve never seen a Sin-Eater do it. They also don\u2019t believe they come back from the dead but they do believe they can grow whole new brains\u2026 well I suppose that makes sense if you assume that consciousness is nothing more than neurons firing. But then, they also believe in ghosts. This one leaves me feeling a bit confused; every individual belief makes sense but put them together and they clash.<br /> <br /> <b>The Union - </b>We have a compact lumping Sin-Eaters in with witches. Honestly, I\u2019m suprised this hasn\u2019t happened before. Witches are a very diverse lot and from an outside perspective Sin-Eaters could fit in the standard distribution. Then again, there\u2019s solid out of character reasons everyone thinks of Sin-Eaters as a separate concept. Despite this, a lot of The Unions response hinges around the Sin-Eater\u2019s ability to resurrection, which is an oddly specific bit of knowledge for them to have if they think Sin-Eaters are witches. Apart from that this is what you\u2019d expect. The union worries about if Sin-Eaters are threats, and sometimes form alliances with them. I did particularly like the fact some Union Cells wondered if they need to protect the local ghosts as well as the local people.<br /> <br /> <b>Ageis Kai Doru - </b>As straight forward as it can be, the AKD doesn\u2019t really care about Sin-Eaters unless they go of the deep end. They occasionally want their Relics, but not too much because they rarely have useful powers for humans and those which do are really rare. Sometimes they want to explore the Underworld for lore and relics and don\u2019t object to bringing a Sin-Eater into the expedition. Unlike the Changeling section, this one presents the AKD as fully aware that Sin-Eaters aren\u2019t witches, I\u2019m fine with that since I like to portray the AKD as really knowledgeable - though I\u2019m sure that some mistakes will be made.<br /> <br /> <b>The Ascending Ones - </b>So this section talks about both the Underworld and the Sin-Eaters. The Jagged Crescent have industrial scale harvesting to make \u201cDeath Cloud\u201d (I\u2019m not sure if they want to sell it, use it in the vigil or both) while other Hunters might travel there for specific regents. As for Sin-Eaters, they have a dogma (powerful would be dictator; like back in ancient egypt) which many hunters on the ground disagree with. It\u2019s a neat dynamic, but I\u2019ll probably tweak it so that the elders know that Sin-Eaters get massively more powerful in a Tier 3 Krewe, while hunters on the ground are looking at what Sin-Eaters are now and not what they might become.<br /> <br /> <b>The Cherion Group - </b>This section was a mostly a history one which can be boiled down to the fact that Cherion hasn\u2019t had much success with Sin-Eaters.<br /> <br /> <b>The Lucifuge - </b>I like it when the Lucifuge know their stuff about demons, so I liked the angle here: Sin-Eaters claim divine or demonic patrons, the Lucifuge know they don\u2019t have them. This is probably correct. They also don\u2019t consider the Underworld to be cosmologically significant, but they\u2019re keeping an open mind. There\u2019s also a good variety of opinions presented about what hunters think about Ghostwalkers.<br /> <br /> <b>The Malleus Maleficarum - </b>Catholic theology says that everything supernatural comes from God or Satan; so naturally the MM picked one of the two. They said they\u2019re possessed by demons. The response bugs me a bit though, we know that the MM performs exorcisms all the time (they have a Benediction for it in the corebook), and presumably they don\u2019t just kill the people they freed. Yet the MM says Sin-Eaters are not a special case. Even weirder, when the Order of St. Ambrose suggests that maybe they shouldn\u2019t be killing Sin-Eaters, the rest of the conspiracy doesn\u2019t listen because \u201cIt seems that most Malleus hunters don\u2019t want to overreact<br /> and condemn innocents, not without hard proof\u201d. I think the author changed his mind at some point, and didn\u2019t finish updating everything. I hope the intent was that the Malleus treats them as possessed innocents they can\u2019t save. It\u2019s a nice mixture of merciful and fanatical that\u2019s more interesting than just pure kill-em-all fanaticism.<br /> <br /> <b>Task Force: VALKRIE - </b>This one is straight forward. TF:V tends to think that Sin-Eaters are dangerous, but the higher ups want to use them for access to the underworld, at least until they have a technological replacement. They want into the Underworld both for a strategic advantage and to take out monsters at the source.<br /> <br /> <br /> Overall thoughts. Most of the sections here try to draw a link between one aspect of the Compact/Conspiracy with one aspect of the Sin-Eater line; with some more general material on the side. Like TF:V linking to the Underworld. For the most part I liked these sections and think basing things around a core link is a good idea. It also allowed a lot more sections to mention different sub-groups, which I think worked well.<br /> <br /> The weakness is that in a few places these more complex portrayals occasionally led to bits that were self contradictory.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Wandering Monsters</b><br /> <br /> We only get one Sin-Eater this time; a Torn or Forgotten (Probably Torn) Advocate who can either aid hunters with ghosts or cause problems because her Geist likes her to start fires. All in all a typical Sin-Eater, but with only one portrayed that\u2019s probably a good idea.<br /> <br /> The Story-Hooks present perhaps the three most typical Sin-Eater / Hunter stories. The two trying to do the same job, a Sin-Eater who\u2019s \u201cnoisy\u201d but not actively hurting anyone (at the moment) and a Sin-Eater who\u2019s killing. I did like the way the three Tiers were presented, but I felt the story about the con artist ignored all possibilities other than violence to it\u2019s detrement.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-05T23:56:27-07:00","id":126501,"title":"","content":"Glad you enjoyed it.<br /> <br /> I played up the rarity partially as, well, Sin-Eaters are both really obvious with their powers and pretty groundbreaking. They are hard to ignore if you know about em, but the WoD in general seems far less aware of them than Vampires, Werewolves, Mages, even Promethean.<br /> <br /> I wrote fiction snippets but they were cut for space. A shame, really, but while they were fun I needed more space for the Response.<br /> <br /> I tried to not make it look like there was a pogrom on AA. It's more a matter of Sin-Eaters are wary of them, and word on the street/web is they are bad news. A Storyteller is welcome to take that in a dire direction, but AA is not going the way of the dodo anytime soon...well, not due to Sin-Eaters, anyway.<br /> <br /> With AKD, while some groups kinda freaked out about Sin-Eaters I wanted to specifically show that at least one major group looked at them and said &quot;Meh&quot;, not because they are unaware of their implications, but because in terms of their goals, they frankly don't matter.<br /> <br /> I've have happily written several more paragraphs on the Loyalists of Thule and Sin-Eaters.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampi